{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices across most horizons; apply an upward level shift (larger in the first ~1–20 days, still present out to 30d).",
    "Trend/acceleration underreaction: when the recent history turns up, the model lags and fails to capture continued upside momentum; add a positive momentum term proportional to the last 5–10d slope.",
    "Peak/trough timing lag: model often predicts local highs/lows a few days late/early relative to outcomes; shift turning points earlier (i.e., accelerate the response) in rising regimes.",
    "Excess mean reversion in forecasts: model pulls prices back toward the recent mean too aggressively; dampen mean-reversion strength, especially after upside breakouts.",
    "Horizon shape error: near-term (≈days 1–15) underpricing is strongest; use a decaying correction (bigger uplift near-term, smaller but nonzero by day 30).",
    "Occasional late-horizon downside overshoot: in some cases the model drifts too low by days ~25–30 while realized stays elevated; impose a floor tied to recent realized level (e.g., not below trailing 10d average minus a small band)."
  ]
}
